  "Question": "Given \\(2^m=3\\) and \\(2^n=5\\), find the value of \\(2^{m+n+1}\\).",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "30",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "If \\(m,n\\) are restricted to positive integers, as in the stated Grade 8 unit, no such \\(m,n\\) exist: every positive integer power of \\(2\\) is even, whereas \\(3\\) and \\(5\\) are odd.\n\nIf positive-base real powers are intended, splitting the exponent lets us use both given values directly. The real-exponent sum law gives\n\n\\(2^{m+n+1}=2^m\\cdot2^n\\cdot2=3\\times5\\times2=30\\).",
